  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...One last day of unsettled weather before a \ntranquil stretch takes over. \n\nA closed low over the north continues to slowly move off to the\neast/northeast, with our area remaining under cyclonic flow. \nAnother shortwave will rotate through later this afternoon, \ntriggering another round of scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms this afternoon. Not expecting any severe weather \nor flooding concerns overall though and this should be the final\nday of active and unsettled weather until Sunday. Some areas \nlikely stay dry as the surface low slowly pulls away. Thinking a\nlarge majority of showers will be east of a line from Cape May \nto Philadelphia to Mount Pocono. Temperatures will continue to \nbe below normal for late July, topping out in the upper 70s/low\nto mid 80s. \n\nHigh pressure moves in tonight, with a stretch of dry weather\nexpected through Saturday Night. Temperatures begin to moderate\ntowards seasonal averages as we get into the end of the week and\ninto the weekend. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A frontal system on Sunday and Monday will \nbring the next chance for showers and thunderstorms after today.\n\nHigh pressure weakens late Saturday and is replaced by \nadvancing low pressure from the Ohio Valley area. This low \nstrengthens and moves west of our area later Sunday and into \nMonday. Thus system will bring increasing chances for showers \nand a few tstms Sunday afternoon for the western counties and \nall areas Sunday night and Monday. The PWAT values off the \nforecast soundings during these times show more 2+ inch PWAT \nvalues for our region. So it's possible that excessive rainfall \nmay develop for parts of the region. Moisture and instability\nwill also increase ahead of the front, so we likely will have to\nmonitor the severe weather threat for the Sunday and Monday \nafternoon period. However, our area is not in any long term \noutlooks, so there is a chance that there are more hydro\nconcerns with this system.",
